512: # --------------------------------------------------- the CROSS-LATCH row
513: #  *** EVERY ROW ABOVE RUNS IN A FRESH SESSION WITH NO PRIOR DEVICE TRAFFIC, AND A
514: #  REVIEW SEAT POINTED OUT WHAT THAT CANNOT SEE: a bit-value mutant onto an
515: #  ALREADY-USED bit (1 << 6 is #498's SGIRE_W_DE_LEN, 1 << 8 is #499's UNMAP_RD)
516: #  leaves those latches CLEAR in a fresh session, so the announce still fires and
517: #  every row above passes.  The collision is only visible once something else has
518: #  ALREADY SET the colliding bit. ***
519: #
520: #  It matters because the four sgi_re/sgi_de/sgi_mte/sgi_de_status handlers share
521: #  ONE malloc'd struct, so `d->warned' is shared ACROSS DEVICES -- #498 measured a
522: #  reused bit at 23/25, and BOTH #499 and this round were originally designed for
523: #  1 << 7.  The collision was caught by a human listing the enum, which is exactly
524: #  the kind of catch that should not be relied upon.
525: #
526: #  So: fire the #498 width latch FIRST with a byte access, THEN trigger the draw,
527: #  and require the prim-op decline ANYWAY.  Under a colliding bit the first access
528: #  pre-sets it and this row goes silent.
529: A = Addr(CODE)
530: t, alive, how = session(store(A, DE + 0x0, val="0x5a", op=OP_SB_T0_A3)
531:                         + store(A, DE + PRIM_START, val="0x02000000")
532:                         + load(A, 0xffffffff80002000) + ["print v0"])
